WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.840
Hollywood is an ever-changing landscape, and
nobody knows that better than action stars

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.020
of years gone by.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:11.420
From world karate champions to kung fu prodigies,
these actors were born to be action heroes,

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.140
but for one reason or another, they all wore
out their welcome in the movie business.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.900
Michael Dudikoff spent years feeding off bit
parts before he won the role of Private Joe

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:25.020
T. Armstrong in 1985's American Ninja, which
marked the beginning of his time as the golden

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.460
boy of Cannon Films.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.560
In the years that followed, Cannon’s owners
managed to keep him at the B-film studio by

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.900
promising him all kinds of meaty roles.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.620
Dudikoff told Coming Soon,

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.020
“There were so many things I was told I
was going to be.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.820
I was going to be Superman; I was going to
be Spider-Man…

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.060
That never did happen, but they were working
on it.”

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.770
Instead of playing superheroes, he fronted
such schlock classics as Avenging Force and

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.420
Platoon Leader in the years following American
Ninja.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.540
Cannon bought the rights to Superman at a
steal, but they managed to convince Christopher

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:58.120
Reeve to reprise the role in the critically
panned: Superman IV: The Quest for Peace.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.840
Dudikoff went on to make more American Ninja
movies, which he remains best known for today.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:03.840
In his own words,

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.940
"I'd like to work a little more, but I'm here."

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.740
When Danish model Brigitte Nielsen was offered
the chance to transition into movies, she

00:01:10.741 --> 00:01:11.940
had a tough decision to make.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.900
Her first marriage was over and she had a
young child to look after, but the part was

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.100
too big to decline.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:18.340
She told The Guardian,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.000
"I let my parents bring up my son Julian to
pursue my Hollywood dream…

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.160
I got the opportunity to appear in a movie
called Red Sonja with Arnold Schwarzenegger."

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:31.100
Soon after portraying the sword-wielding Marvel
heroine, Nielsen appeared in Rocky IV with

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.620
Schwarzenegger's rival, Sylvester Stallone.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:38.660
Sly became her second husband and they starred
alongside each other in 1986's Cobra before

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:39.780
calling it quits.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.570
The statuesque blonde went on to appear in
1987's Beverly Hills Cop II and fronted a

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.960
number of largely forgettable action movies
throughout the '90s, but the A-list status

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.580
she coveted in those early days slowly slipped
away.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.060
Today, Nielsen is best known for her stints
on various reality TV shows and recently made

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:01.020
headlines in June 2018, when she gave birth
to her fifth child at the age of 54.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.940
"And I am the happiest mother.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.880
I’m probably the happiest I’ve ever been."

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.860
She might not be getting the biggest roles
anymore, but she remains a superwoman in real

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:09.360
life.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.019
Blade certainly wasn't perfect, but Wesley
Snipes' charisma and physical prowess turned

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:18.249
the Marvel vampire movie into a cult classic
that would spawn two sequels, one that was

00:02:18.249 --> 00:02:21.360
relatively enjoyable, and one that was downright
awful.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.380
Snipes' behavior on the set of Blade: Trinity
is now a thing of legend.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.340
Speaking to AV Club, Patton Oswalt revealed
how the leading man fell out with director

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.060
David S. Goyer big time.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.260
"The fact that that movie exists, puts it
above Citizen Kane."

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.739
According to Oswalt, things got so bad that
Snipes would apparently only communicate with

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:43.560
the director through Post-It notes, signing
off each one with "From Blade."

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.660
On-set feuds turned out to be the least of
Snipes' worries.

00:02:46.669 --> 00:02:51.120
He wound up serving two years behind bars
after he was found guilty of tax evasion,

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.620
bringing his time as an A-list action star
to an end.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.200
He had a stint as a linebacker for the Oakland
Raiders in the early '70s, but Carl Weathers

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.620
retired from football after catching the acting
bug.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:04.760
He paid his dues in small roles for film and
television until Rocky came along.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:09.220
His character, flamboyant boxer Apollo Creed
continued to pop up in the franchise until

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:12.320
his death in the ring in 1985's Rocky IV.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.980
The death of Apollo Creed didn't mark the
end for Weathers, however.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.000
He went on to appear alongside Arnold Schwarzenegger
in 1987's Predator.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:24.480
He led his very own action outing the following
year with Action Jackson, but the movie was

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.240
torn to shreds by the critics.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.420
Weathers defended the film in a 2009 interview
with Bullz-Eye, saying interview with

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.090
"I still maintain today that it could've been
a better movie had we taken a little bit longer

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:35.420
to develop the screenplay.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.020
A lot of people liked it.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.460
In Germany, it was huge!"

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.480
Success in Europe wasn't enough to convince
the studio to make a sequel, however, and

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.240
a planned Action Jackson franchise was canned.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.980
Weathers still makes a living as a character
actor today,

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.320
"His name is Carl Weathers, and he’s my
new acting teacher."

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.540
...but his time as an action hero is well
and truly over.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:59.560
Jet Li was just 11 years old when a producer
stumbled across him giving a kung fu demonstration

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:00.300
in Hong Kong.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.580
Impressed with Li's skills, the producer came
back to visit him every year to check his

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.080
progress.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.480
He was 17 when the exec decided he was old
enough to start making movies, and before

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.720
long, he was a star in his native China.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.599
Li's first American movie was 1998's Lethal
Weapon 4.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.960
He played a villain, but he didn't have to
wait long for his first hero role.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.280
In 2000, Romeo Must Die was a modest hit for
Li.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.160
The tragic death of Li's co-star Aaliyah put
the idea of a proposed sequel to bed.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.440
Li remained a hot property in Hollywood for
a while, and was even offered a role in the

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.860
Matrix franchise, but the actor and his family
were almost killed in the Boxing Day tsunami

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:36.940
of 2004.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.960
Seeing the destruction first hand made him
want to dedicate more time to philanthropy

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:41.960
rather than filmmaking.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.960
After struggling through school with undiagnosed
dyslexia, Billy Blanks turned away from the

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.340
books and started to focus on martial arts.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.600
If he couldn't succeed academically he wanted
to excel at karate instead.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:54.680
He told CNN,

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.599
"Sooner or later, I started to see myself
progress.

00:04:57.599 --> 00:05:01.020
The next thing you know, I got my black belt
before everybody in the class."

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.750
He went on to become a seven-time world karate
champion and attained black belts in several

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.600
other martial arts before he decided he needed
a new challenge: Hollywood.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.820
Blanks had the fighting skills to make it
as an action star, but his trouble with reading

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.000
proved problematic.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.600
Luckily, his wife helped him memorize scripts
and soon enough, Blanks was rubbing shoulders

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:23.990
with some of action’s biggest stars, appearing
in Jet Li's The Master, Jean-Claude Van Damme's

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.720
A.W.O.L, and the Bruce Willis-led action comedy
The Last Boy Scout.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:34.460
In 1992 he was finally rewarded with his first
lead part, though Talons of the Eagle wasn't

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:36.300
exactly star-maker material.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:40.080
Hollywood forgot about Blanks over the years
that followed, and today he's best-known as

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.580
the inventor of Tae Bo, a total body fitness
system that he apparently came up with while

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:45.900
listening to the Rocky soundtrack.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.540
He could have been a household name today,
but Kenpō expert Jeff Speakman failed to

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:55.600
capitalize on his "strong and entertaining"
debut in 1991's The Perfect Weapon.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.580
Speakman had a three-picture deal with Paramount,
but changes were afoot within the studio,

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.319
and the project that was being developed as
his follow-up vehicle was accidentally sold

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.260
to Fox, who refused to sell it back.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.060
Speakman told The Action Elite,

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.740
"They made that movie, which was supposed
to be my second movie at Paramount, and the

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.360
name of that movie was Speed.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.380
It was the movie that made Keanu Reeves a
star...We lost it.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.660
And consequently, there went my career."

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.980
The movie that Speakman wound up doing instead
two years later was Street Knight.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.160
Two years might not seem like a significant
absence, but countless similar movies came

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.200
out in that time.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.800
Street Knight got lost in the noise, and so
did Speakman, who stopped acting in 2006.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.280
He was diagnosed with throat cancer in 2013,
but he did what he does best and fought it:

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.300
by 2014, he'd been given the all clear.

